A3b — Every Claims-related tag, across all namespaces
fetch dt.entity.host
| fields id, entity.name, tags
| expand tags
| filter contains(lower(tags), "claim")
| summarize hosts = countDistinctExact(id), by:{tags}
| sort hosts desc


This strips out portfolio/product noise and shows only Claims. Watch for Portfolio:Claims, [Azure]Portfolio:Claims, [AWS]Portfolio:Claims alongside the CMDB one.

A3c — True distinct Claims host count (dedupe across namespaces)
fetch dt.entity.host
| fields id, entity.name, tags
| expand tags
| filter contains(lower(tags), "claim")
| summarize hosts = countDistinctExact(id)


If this returns 82, the CMDB tag is complete. If it returns more, multiple namespaces are in play and every rollup must filter on all of them.

A2b — Which APM ID tag key exists (this is the critical one)
fetch dt.entity.host
| fields id, entity.name, tags
| expand tags
| filter contains(lower(tags), "apm")
   or contains(tags, "APP000")
   or contains(lower(tags), "npid")
| summarize hosts = countDistinctExact(id), by:{tags}
| sort hosts desc
| limit 200


A1/A3 didn't surface an APM ID tag in the top 200, which is expected — APM IDs are high-cardinality so each value has few hosts and they sink below the cutoff. This filters directly for them.

A3d — All tag keys present on Claims hosts specifically
fetch dt.entity.host
| fields id, entity.name, tags
| filter iArrayContains(tags, "CMDB_Portfolio:Claims")
| expand tags
| summarize hosts = countDistinctExact(id), by:{tags}
| sort hosts desc
| limit 300


